Burn Operating System (BurnOS)
==============================

Repository Description
----------------------

Welcome to the official repository for BurnOS, an operating system
written entirely in C that is based on neither Unix nor Linux.

License
-------

BurnOS is free software released under the terms of the 3-Clause BSD License.
See the LICENSE file for details.

Quick Start
-----------

To quickly build, package, and test BurnOS on your local machine,
ensure you have Docker and QEMU.

1. Clone the repository
   git clone https://github.com/FroggyAwesome/BurnOS.git
   cd BurnOS

2. Build the kernel, create the ISO image, and run it in QEMU
   make docker run

Project Layout
--------------

The source tree is intentionally kept minimalist and flat where possible:

  arch/   - Low-level configurations
  bin/    - Binaries
  boot/   - Boot configurations
  dev/    - Hardware drivers
  etc/    - Configurations
  kernel/ - Core kernel

Languages & Compilers Used
--------------------------

- C99 (GNU C Compiler)
- Assembly (GNU Assembler)
- Linker Script (GNU Linker)

Tools Used
----------

- Docker
- GDB (GNU Debugger)
- Make (GNU Make)
- Vim

Hardware Requirements
---------------------

- CPU - Intel 386/486/586/686
- GPU - iGPU
- RAM - 1M
- KB  - PS2 USA
- FW  - BIOS

Development & Contribution Workflow
-----------------------------------

Before submitting any Pull Request or patch to BurnOS, code must pass
the strict verification pipeline to ensure compliance with the target
architecture and formatting standards.

1. Verify code syntax and warnings (treating all warnings as errors):
   make check

2. Format the codebase automatically using the project's formatting configuration:
   make format

3. Test the operating system in debug mode to ensure stability and proper execution:
   make debug iso run

Unformatted code, failing static checks, or runtime regressions will be automatically rejected.

AI Coding Assistant Guidelines
------------------------------

Code generated or assisted by AI tools must strictly adhere to the
following constraints before being proposed:

- Environment: 32-bit x86 bare-metal, freestanding. No standard C library.
- Language Standard: Strict GNU C99.
- Philosophy: Favor pure, low-level, self-contained implementations
  over complex abstractions or unnecessary dependencies.

Communication & Support
-----------------------

For questions, discussions, bug reports, or feature proposals regarding BurnOS,
please open an issue or submit a pull request on GitHub.

Please avoid opening issues for general C programming questions or personal
operating system design support. Keep discussions focused strictly on the BurnOS codebase.
